1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:40,280
Hello there, welcome to the final chapter.
This is also the final deliverable of my
C++ advanced Deep Dive, so let us make it fun.
Below are five chips holding our shared numbers:
an 8-byte buffer, a 15-letter input, and the 16-byte write.
Because the red program was intentionally vulnerable,
we only ran it in an isolated lab.
Which makes everything in this replay safe to touch.

2
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:40,264
Now, let us get a bit interactive with this lab replay.
Let us play around with the letters in the box
and see what happens.
Watch all three panels update together.
The blue panel caps the view.
The red panel counts how many bytes pass our 8-byte buffer.
And the green panel keeps at most 7 letters plus null.
One cause, three effects. So the comparison is direct.

3
00:01:44,000 --> 00:02:14,694
Let us meet the four shapes of the same bug.
Each card hides a copy with no size check:
strcpy, strcat, scanf, and memcpy with the wrong size.
Go on, click each card and see what pops out.
Each one reveals why it writes past the buffer,
and the checked form that replaces it.

4
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:51,680
Now meet the good guys.
These are the tools that carry their size with them.
Strings grow themselves. Views borrow with bounds.
snprintf prints at most n.
And memcpy plus min seals with null.
Poke the chips below and see how sanitizers
and scans back up that same rule.

5
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:27,235
Time to test ourselves with four quick checks.
Each card asks one thing: who frees the heap,
how much passes the boundary, what the strcpy flaw is,
and what WRITE 16 means.
Take a guess, pick an answer, and see if you were right.
The reason appears right below.

6
00:03:31,000 --> 00:04:22,880
Now the fun part.
Let us fix three short snippets with the habits above.
Copy a name with min plus null.
Read a word with percent-7-s.
Own memory with make unique.
Watch the verdict line at the bottom.
It turns green only when all three are correct.

7
00:04:24,000 --> 00:05:04,907
And we close with the layers behind safe code,
and the full arc.
Phase 1 showed the line. Phase 2 broke it.
Phase 3 held it. And the Final replays all three, and holds.
The chips repeat our numbers: 8, 15, 16 blocked,
offset 40, hex 41 to 4F, exit zero.
Thank you for playing along.
